From bc6a8f61a825e12e4e6e6de8d4f3013d5d715a18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kel Modderman <kelvmod@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:59:07 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] [network] Signal WS-Discovery to stop with the other servers,
 join later

When the network services go down, CNetworkServices::Stop() runs twice:
first with bWait false to tell each server to stop, then with bWait true
to wait for them. StopWSDiscovery() had no bWait and joined the posix
listener thread in the first call. That thread only looks for a stop
request when its select() times out, every 3 seconds, so the main thread
sat in the join for up to 3 seconds before it even logged "Waiting for
network services to stop". On exit, the service add-ons are stopped
right after that.

Timing Kodi's exit on Linux with many Python service add-ons, 1.2 to
2.6 s went by between "Stopping all" and the first
"CServiceAddonManager: stopping" line. In one of those logs the event
server thread ended 537 ms and the TCP server thread 990 ms after
"Signaling network services to stop", the WS-Discovery thread 2589 ms
after it, and the main thread logged "CWSDiscoveryListenerUDP::Stop -
Stopped" and stopped the first add-on 2590 ms after it.

Give StopWSDiscovery(), IWSDiscovery::StopServices() and
CWSDiscoveryListenerUDP::Stop() a bWait argument, as the other servers
have. The first call now only signals the listener, and the second joins
it and logs "Stopped". Only that join logs it, so the destructor's own
stop at exit no longer logs it a second time. If the listener has
already ended by the second call, the destructor joins it later, as
happens for the event server.

That alone would only move the wait, so the listener's select() now
times out after 1 second, as CEventServer's and CTCPServer's do. All
three then see the stop request within about a second of the first
call, and the second call waits for them together.

The listener still sends its Bye before it ends. Its other waits already
watch for the stop: CThread::Sleep() in DispatchCommand() returns as
soon as the thread is told to stop, and the sendto() retry loop checks
m_bStop. Turning WS-Discovery off in the settings still stops it and
waits. CWSDiscoveryWindows has no thread of its own, so it ignores bWait
and still releases the WSDAPI provider in the first call; the second
call finds nothing left to do.

Assisted-by: Claude
(cherry picked from commit 85c5a5ea6dd78770acd516f4b242c95cdbf7d7f8)
